WEBVTT

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.180
Fifteen products. One chip.

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:05.180
This is the long version.

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:10.180
Every SKU in the plan, what it physically is, how it runs on shared silicon,

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:13.180
who pays for it, and what it contributes.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:17.180
Everything here is a management projection prepared for fundraising.

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:21.180
Each product gets the same four slides, in the same order.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:27.180
What it is, how it works, who buys it and why now, and how it fits.

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:30.180
Four of them get two more, because a working simulator exists,

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:33.180
and you should watch it rather than take our word.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:38.180
Learn the pattern once, and the next eighty slides navigate themselves.

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:41.180
The demand is legislated, not forecast.

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:45.180
Driver drowsiness, blind spot, and lane departure obligations

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:49.180
land on new and existing commercial vehicles, on fixed dates.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:52.180
About a million trucks a year fall in scope.

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:55.180
For a fleet operator, the question is not whether to buy,

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:58.180
it is whose chip is inside when they do.

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:03.180
The whole plan is downstream of one twenty-eight nanometer part,

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:05.180
and the program is deliberately gated.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:11.180
Prove the compute and radar blocks first, then the full die, then back-end sign-off.

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:14.180
The production mask, the largest single commitment,

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:17.180
is only paid for once working silicon exists.

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:19.180
Here is the part itself.

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:26.180
32,768 multiply accumulate units at 600 megahertz,

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:29.180
102 gigabytes a second of memory bandwidth,

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:34.180
hardware softmax, and lockstep cores for the automotive safety path.

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:38.180
The number that matters most, it fuses eleven sensor channels

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:42.180
in 8.6 milliseconds of a 33 millisecond frame.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:45.180
74% of the frame is left unused,

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:49.180
and that headroom is why one die becomes fifteen products.

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:53.180
All fifteen, ranked by revenue in the final year.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:56.180
Places are held flat across six years,

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:59.180
so growth comes from volume, not from price.

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:02.180
Note how far the list ranges.

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:04.180
50,000 rupees for a mirror unit,

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:07.180
18,000 for a bare die,

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:09.180
several lakh for a truck kit,

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:13.180
same silicon, priced independently by the market it sells into.

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:17.180
Road autonomy is two-thirds of the plan,

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:21.180
762 crore, three SKUs.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.180
This is where the mandate acts,

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:25.180
and where the concentration risk sits.

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:27.180
Take these three carefully.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:30.180
A replacement rear-view mirror with a display built in,

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:33.180
four small body cameras, and a loom.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:37.180
50,000 rupees, fitted in an afternoon.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:39.180
It is the least sophisticated thing we make,

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:42.180
and it ships first on current generation hardware.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:46.180
Four cameras cover the quarters a driver cannot see.

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:49.180
The chip stitches them into a single surround view,

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:52.180
and watches the danger zone for anything entering it.

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:56.180
The driver gets a stitched image and a directional chime.

00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:58.180
No braking, no steering.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:01.180
It informs, and that is the whole product.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:04.180
No mandate applies here, which is exactly the point.

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:07.180
It already sells on its own merits.

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:10.180
Owners and small fleets buy it as an accessory,

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:13.180
through fitment shops and accessory catalogs,

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:15.180
for visibility and parking.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:18.180
Demand does not wait for a compliance date.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:22.180
270 crore, 24% of group revenue,

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:25.180
at 89% gross margin.

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:29.180
It carries the highest unit count of anything in the portfolio,

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:31.180
and the lowest fitment complexity.

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:35.180
So it builds the channel reach that the harder products inherit later.

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:39.180
A self-driving retrofit kit for vehicles that never leave a building.

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:43.180
A sensor head, a compute box, and a drive-by-wire interface.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:45.180
One lakh rupees.

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:48.180
Fitted to forklifts and tugs a sight already owns.

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:53.180
This is the first product where the silicon closes the loop instead of advising.

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:58.180
Cameras and depth sensing read the aisle, the load, and the people in it.

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:01.180
The chip localizes against a sight map it holds on board.

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:04.180
A planner picks a path and a speed,

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:08.180
then drive-by-wire actually steers, accelerates, and brakes,

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:12.180
the vehicle drives, the driver is not there.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:18.180
Warehouse and industrial operators, direct or through material handling integrators.

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:21.180
Inside a private site, no homologation applies,

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:25.180
so this is a commercial decision, not a regulatory one,

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:31.180
which is why level four behavior ships here years before it is legal on a public road.

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:35.180
42 crore, under 4% of revenue,

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:38.180
small in the model and large in the argument.

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:41.180
It is the technical proof point for the road roadmap,

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:45.180
running the same silicon in a place where it is allowed to make the decision.

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:49.180
We built a drawable warehouse floor for this.

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:55.180
You author the route, place workers, pure vehicles, trolleys, and spills as obstacles,

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:56.180
and run it.

00:04:56.180 --> 00:04:58.180
The alert policy is configurable.

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:04.180
Honk, voice, both, silent, because that is a sight decision, not ours.

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:06.180
And recovery is explicit.

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:09.180
It reroutes after a set stuck time.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:11.180
Here it is, actually running.

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:16.180
Inbound put away, then a pick run, then the peak hour floor.

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:20.180
Watch the safety ring traveling with the truck and gating at speed.

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:25.180
And watch the sensor fusion panel, top right, build the bird's eye view.

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:29.180
The third scenario is the one that decides whether a site can run this.

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:35.180
The smart truck kit, forward camera, radar, compute box, driver display,

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:38.180
and the harness that talks to the vehicle.

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:40.180
Two and a half lakh rupees.

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:45.180
This is the biggest single line in the plan and the one the mandate is written for.

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:49.180
Camera and radar watch the forward path and the blind quarters.

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:54.180
The chip fuses both into one object list carrying range and closing speed.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:59.180
Collision, lane, and blind spot logic decide whether to warn.

00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:04.180
The driver is warned and where the vehicle supports it, the brake interface is signaled.

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:10.180
Radar matters here because closing speed is what a braking decision actually turns on.

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:13.180
Fleet operators and truck manufacturers.

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:16.180
And the decision is compliance led rather than discretionary.

00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:19.180
Retrofit through installers now.

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:22.180
Factory fit is the longer, larger route.

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:26.180
The buying question is which supplier on what date?

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:28.180
450 crore.

00:06:28.180 --> 00:06:31.180
40% of group revenue from one SKU.

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:34.180
That is the concentration to underwrite.

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:39.180
It is also why the portfolio has a demand floor at all rather than a demand forecast.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:41.180
Three road profiles.

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:48.180
Market street, single carriageway highway, and a deliberate stress test run against the same perception stack.

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:50.180
The vehicle model is real.

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:56.180
550 kilograms, 2.1 meter wheelbase, tire friction of 0.75.

00:06:56.180 --> 00:07:01.180
Steering angle and lateral acceleration are solved, not scripted.

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:03.180
Watch the planner view in the center.

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:08.180
It redraws from the fused object list, not from an animation path.

00:07:08.180 --> 00:07:13.180
On the right, the perception rail names what it sees and how far away it is.

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:17.180
A motorbike at 26 meters, an auto rickshaw at 38.

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:22.180
Three road profiles, one stack, nothing reloaded between them.

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:27.180
Before the silicon section, look at what the truck kit actually hands the compute.

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:29.180
Seven color cameras.

00:07:29.180 --> 00:07:36.180
Road left and right, side left and right, a dash cam, a driver monitoring camera, and a rear camera.

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:39.180
Two thermal cameras, one in each mirror tower.

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:43.180
Radar at both ends for range and relative motion.

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:50.180
11 configured inputs arriving at one incab processor and one perception platform leaving it.

00:07:50.180 --> 00:07:56.180
For the fleet operator, the installation is a single application, not 11 integrations.

00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:58.180
And here it is moving.

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:04.180
Watch the 11 channels converge on the die, and one AD2 stream leave it.

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:10.180
The bar underneath is the frame budget, 8.6 milliseconds against 33.3.

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:13.180
Treat that as an architecture illustration.

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:18.180
The next two slides set out exactly what would have to be measured before it becomes a claim.

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:20.180
The 11 are not redundancy.

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:26.180
Each tower carries a road camera, a 120 degree side camera, and a thermal camera.

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:30.180
So the same quarter is seen three ways in different physics.

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:35.180
Radar covers both ends, where cameras are weakest in rain and glare.

00:08:35.180 --> 00:08:41.180
Inside the cab, the dash cam records, and the driver monitoring camera watches the driver.

00:08:41.180 --> 00:08:43.180
Different tasks on different data.

00:08:43.180 --> 00:08:49.180
What stays open is calibration, occlusion, mounting, and the final sensor list.

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:53.180
Before we leave the truck kit, look at the sensor layout it actually ships.

00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:57.180
Two towers retrofit above the existing mirrors.

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:02.180
Each with a thermal camera, a road camera, and a 120 degree side camera.

00:09:02.180 --> 00:09:06.180
Two cameras in the cab, 4D radar, front and rear.

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:09.180
Watch the coverage cone sweep and overlap.

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:14.180
The claim the layout makes is that no quarter of a heavy truck goes unobserved.

00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:17.180
This is the discipline that number still needs.

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:21.180
33.3 milliseconds is about 30 frames a second.

00:09:21.180 --> 00:09:26.180
8.6 of them is the processing claim, which leaves roughly 24.7.

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:30.180
But that is budget subtraction, not measured headroom.

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:33.180
Three things have to be fixed before it is a claim.

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:38.180
Which acquisition, inference, and fusion stages sit inside the measurement,

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:42.180
the precision and sustained rate, and the worst case rather than the average.

00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:47.180
The proving is staged because the stages answer different questions.

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:53.180
An FPGA demonstration exercises the software, the interfaces, and prototype compute.

00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:59.180
ASIC verification checks scaled compute, memory, clock, power, and area together.

00:09:59.180 --> 00:10:02.180
None of which an FPGA can tell you.

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:07.180
Vehicle qualification tests thermal behavior, the safety path, and real operating conditions.

00:10:07.180 --> 00:10:12.180
Confidence should track the evidence at each stage rather than run ahead of it.

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:15.180
That staging already has a history.

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:18.180
Nine steps, and five of them are silicon.

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:23.180
Each rung added one block and then measured the frame rate that followed.

00:10:23.180 --> 00:10:26.180
Watch the bars fill as it climbs.

00:10:26.180 --> 00:10:29.180
So C2 is what this raise funds.

00:10:29.180 --> 00:10:35.180
32,768 multiply accumulates at 600 megahertz.

00:10:35.180 --> 00:10:38.180
Tapeout in the fourth quarter of 2026.

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:42.180
The frame rates are management figures, not third-party benchmarks.

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:47.180
Now, the half of the business that sells the chip instead of the vehicle.

00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:53.180
194 crore across five SKUs, and the highest margins in the plan,

00:10:53.180 --> 00:10:55.180
because none of these need an installation crew.

00:10:55.180 --> 00:11:01.180
The bare die, in tray or reel, for a customer to place on their own board.

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:03.180
18,000 rupees.

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:06.180
No enclosure, no cables, no fitting.

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:10.180
We ship silicon and a data sheet, and the customer does the rest.

00:11:10.180 --> 00:11:14.180
The customer's own sensors feed their own board.

00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:18.180
Our die does the perception compute inside their design.

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:21.180
Their software decides what to do with the result,

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:24.180
and their product ships with our silicon inside it.

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:27.180
We supply one stage of someone else's chain.

00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:32.180
Manufacturers who want Indian design silicon and domestic supply,

00:11:32.180 --> 00:11:36.180
rather than an imported part, and who have the engineering to integrate it,

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:40.180
procurement policy is often as decisive as specification here.

00:11:40.180 --> 00:11:46.180
54 crore at 94% gross margin, the highest in the portfolio.

00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:50.180
Every rupee here arrives without installation capacity,

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:53.180
warranty logistics, or a fitment channel behind it.

00:11:53.180 --> 00:11:56.180
Now the half of the business that sells the chip itself,

00:11:56.180 --> 00:11:58.180
and the problem that shapes it,

00:11:58.180 --> 00:12:01.180
eight processing cores behind one dispatch point.

00:12:01.180 --> 00:12:04.180
The cores are not the constraint, the queue is.

00:12:04.180 --> 00:12:08.180
Give each core its own address generation and its own transfers,

00:12:08.180 --> 00:12:10.180
and they proceed independently,

00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:14.180
though parallel requests still compete for finite service capacity.

00:12:14.180 --> 00:12:20.180
The 470 to 85 millisecond figure is a proposal awaiting test bench evidence,

00:12:20.180 --> 00:12:22.180
and the deck says so on the slide.

00:12:22.180 --> 00:12:25.180
Watch the two run side by side.

00:12:25.180 --> 00:12:29.180
On the left, one data engine and seven cores waiting their turn.

00:12:29.180 --> 00:12:33.180
On the right, the same silicon with an engine per core.

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:36.180
The utilization columns are the whole point,

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:40.180
short and flat on the left, filled and held on the right.

00:12:40.180 --> 00:12:44.180
Illustrative dispatch, not a measured application speedup.

00:12:44.180 --> 00:12:48.180
What actually sits inside one of those local engines?

00:12:48.180 --> 00:12:51.180
A descriptor names the transfer parameters and the data layout,

00:12:51.180 --> 00:12:54.180
a three level address engine walks the tensor

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:57.180
without asking a central dispatcher for every step,

00:12:57.180 --> 00:13:01.180
a read FIFO absorbs the gap between when memory serves

00:13:01.180 --> 00:13:03.180
and when compute consumes.

00:13:03.180 --> 00:13:06.180
The cost is roughly 600 lookup tables each,

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:09.180
about 4,800 for eight,

00:13:09.180 --> 00:13:12.180
plus arbitration logic and the firmware to drive it.

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:14.180
Step back from our own design,

00:13:14.180 --> 00:13:18.180
and the useful comparison is not who has a DMA,

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:20.180
it is who schedules the movement,

00:13:20.180 --> 00:13:24.180
where the data sits and what has to cross the chip.

00:13:24.180 --> 00:13:27.180
A shared engine schedules for several cores.

00:13:27.180 --> 00:13:31.180
Local controllers issue requests nearer the compute they serve,

00:13:31.180 --> 00:13:33.180
so more data stays local,

00:13:33.180 --> 00:13:37.180
and then software carries the burden of mapping and coordinating it.

00:13:37.180 --> 00:13:40.180
The outcome is decided by workload results,

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:43.180
compiler mapping and implementation IP,

00:13:43.180 --> 00:13:45.180
not by the data mover alone.

00:13:45.180 --> 00:13:49.180
Six chips, six answers to that question.

00:13:49.180 --> 00:13:52.180
From our own shared DMA arrangement today,

00:13:52.180 --> 00:13:56.180
through mobilized VLIW cores on a shared level 2 cache

00:13:56.180 --> 00:13:59.180
and NVIDIA's brute force parallelism,

00:13:59.180 --> 00:14:01.180
out to Tesla's Dojo,

00:14:01.180 --> 00:14:03.180
where every core holds private SRAM

00:14:03.180 --> 00:14:06.180
and the die carries no DRAM at all.

00:14:06.180 --> 00:14:09.180
Per core DMA against a shared level 2 cache

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:10.180
sits in the middle,

00:14:10.180 --> 00:14:13.180
and that middle is where most of the industry already is.

00:14:13.180 --> 00:14:16.180
Vendor figures here come from public sources.

00:14:16.180 --> 00:14:18.180
This is where the eight times figure comes from,

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:20.180
stated carefully.

00:14:20.180 --> 00:14:25.180
64 multiply accumulate positions process the reduction depth across iterations.

00:14:25.180 --> 00:14:30.180
512 positions express that depth in hardware instead.

00:14:30.180 --> 00:14:33.180
Compare the same k equals 8 tile at the same precision,

00:14:33.180 --> 00:14:36.180
and the arithmetic ratio is 8.

00:14:36.180 --> 00:14:38.180
It is an idealized comparison.

00:14:38.180 --> 00:14:40.180
Pipeline fill, tensor shapes,

00:14:40.180 --> 00:14:43.180
data reuse and memory delivery all still apply.

00:14:43.180 --> 00:14:47.180
And this is what the customer is actually buying, running.

00:14:47.180 --> 00:14:50.180
On the left, the flat 8x8 matrix,

00:14:50.180 --> 00:14:52.180
as it works on the FPGA today,

00:14:52.180 --> 00:14:55.180
64 multiply accumulates a cycle,

00:14:55.180 --> 00:14:58.180
iterating the reduction in software.

00:14:58.180 --> 00:15:02.180
On the right, the cube on 28 nanometer silicon,

00:15:02.180 --> 00:15:04.180
where that reduction becomes hardware depth,

00:15:04.180 --> 00:15:07.180
and all eight layers fire at once.

00:15:07.180 --> 00:15:09.180
Watch the tile counters diverge.

00:15:09.180 --> 00:15:12.180
That gap is the eight times figure,

00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:14.180
shown rather than claimed.

00:15:14.180 --> 00:15:16.180
Which is what the section has been building to.

00:15:16.180 --> 00:15:20.180
More arithmetic increases the demand for data.

00:15:20.180 --> 00:15:21.180
It does not satisfy it.

00:15:21.180 --> 00:15:25.180
Independent controllers remove one serialization point,

00:15:25.180 --> 00:15:29.180
but memory mapping and arbitration still decide which requests complete.

00:15:29.180 --> 00:15:32.180
Compute geometry, data movement,

00:15:32.180 --> 00:15:36.180
and physical implementation are one design problem, not three.

00:15:36.180 --> 00:15:38.180
Measure useful work.

00:15:38.180 --> 00:15:41.180
Peak tops on its own says nothing about utilization.

00:15:41.180 --> 00:15:44.180
Follow a single tile end-to-end.

00:15:44.180 --> 00:15:48.180
External memory feeds the shared on-chip storage.

00:15:48.180 --> 00:15:52.180
Eight banks serve requests according to mapping and arbitration.

00:15:52.180 --> 00:15:56.180
56 local movers each supply their own compute cube.

00:15:56.180 --> 00:15:58.180
The figure quoted here,

00:15:58.180 --> 00:16:01.180
56 times 512 multiply accumulates,

00:16:01.180 --> 00:16:04.180
at 600 megahertz times two operations,

00:16:04.180 --> 00:16:06.180
is an arithmetic peak,

00:16:06.180 --> 00:16:09.180
and the slide is explicit that it is not a benchmark.

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:13.180
And here is the failure mode, with the workload held constant.

00:16:13.180 --> 00:16:17.180
Spread evenly, 56 requests give seven per bank.

00:16:17.180 --> 00:16:19.180
Collide them all onto bank zero,

00:16:19.180 --> 00:16:23.180
and seven banks sit idle while the array stalls.

00:16:23.180 --> 00:16:26.180
Spread the addresses again, and the capacity returns.

00:16:26.180 --> 00:16:29.180
What decides which of those you get is tensor layout,

00:16:29.180 --> 00:16:33.180
address hashing, arbitration, and compiler scheduling.

00:16:33.180 --> 00:16:36.180
And the request counts here are a controlled illustration,

00:16:36.180 --> 00:16:38.180
not measured utilization.

00:16:38.180 --> 00:16:40.180
So the honest close to this section,

00:16:40.180 --> 00:16:44.180
a production advantage is demonstrated by the whole platform,

00:16:44.180 --> 00:16:46.180
never by a multiplier count.

00:16:46.180 --> 00:16:50.180
Freeze the core count, the precision, the clock, and the bandwidth.

00:16:50.180 --> 00:16:55.180
Then measure sustained throughput and worst-case latency on representative tensors,

00:16:55.180 --> 00:17:00.180
with power, area, safety architecture, and silicon status recorded beside them.

00:17:00.180 --> 00:17:05.180
Arithmetic peaks at 600 megahertz are not measured application throughput,

00:17:05.180 --> 00:17:07.180
and this deck does not offer them as such.

00:17:07.180 --> 00:17:10.180
The same die on an M.2 module,

00:17:10.180 --> 00:17:13.180
one camera channel plus the software kit,

00:17:13.180 --> 00:17:17.180
sized to drop into an enclosure and a sensor the customer already owns.

00:17:17.180 --> 00:17:21.180
One camera in, one perception stream out,

00:17:21.180 --> 00:17:23.180
over a standard module interface.

00:17:23.180 --> 00:17:26.180
It is the smallest configuration the part supports,

00:17:26.180 --> 00:17:30.180
and the point is that it is the same part, running less.

00:17:30.180 --> 00:17:34.180
Product teams who want the silicon and the tool chain,

00:17:34.180 --> 00:17:37.180
but do not want to design a board around a bare die.

00:17:37.180 --> 00:17:43.180
It converts a semiconductor sale into a module sale for customers without a hardware team.

00:17:43.180 --> 00:17:47.180
25.5 crore, at 89% margin.

00:17:47.180 --> 00:17:52.180
Modest on its own, it exists to make the silicon reachable by customers

00:17:52.180 --> 00:17:54.180
who would otherwise not buy at all.

00:17:54.180 --> 00:17:59.180
A board-level module with two camera inputs and the software kit,

00:17:59.180 --> 00:18:03.180
25,000 rupees, the middle rung of the compute box ladder,

00:18:03.180 --> 00:18:06.180
and the one that sells in the largest numbers of the three.

00:18:06.180 --> 00:18:10.180
Two channels is where fusion begins to matter.

00:18:10.180 --> 00:18:15.180
The moment you have to reconcile two views of the same scene into one object list,

00:18:15.180 --> 00:18:20.180
it is the first configuration that justifies purpose-built transformer hardware

00:18:20.180 --> 00:18:22.180
over a general purpose processor.

00:18:22.180 --> 00:18:25.180
Systems that need more than a single view,

00:18:25.180 --> 00:18:27.180
but not full surround coverage,

00:18:27.180 --> 00:18:29.180
reversing with side detection,

00:18:29.180 --> 00:18:31.180
a two-camera inspection rig,

00:18:31.180 --> 00:18:34.180
a small autonomous platform,

00:18:34.180 --> 00:18:37.180
buyers who would find a four-channel card wasteful

00:18:37.180 --> 00:18:39.180
and a single-channel insufficient.

00:18:39.180 --> 00:18:44.180
28.8 crore, a step on a ladder rather than a destination,

00:18:44.180 --> 00:18:49.180
but the ladder is what lets one die address several price points at once.

00:18:49.180 --> 00:18:53.180
A PCIe card carrying the chip with four camera inputs

00:18:53.180 --> 00:18:57.180
for a host system that provides the enclosure and the power,

00:18:57.180 --> 00:19:02.180
50,000 rupees, and the largest configuration we sell as a component.

00:19:02.180 --> 00:19:05.180
Four channels demands the memory bandwidth.

00:19:05.180 --> 00:19:08.180
The compute geometry was designed around.

00:19:08.180 --> 00:19:13.180
Each compute cube runs its own local memory access against a banked cache,

00:19:13.180 --> 00:19:17.180
which is what keeps utilization high instead of starving on a single shared path.

00:19:17.180 --> 00:19:22.180
This is the configuration that shows why the die is built the way it is.

00:19:22.180 --> 00:19:25.180
Customers building full surround perception

00:19:25.180 --> 00:19:30.180
who want the compute inside their own architecture rather than in a box we supply,

00:19:30.180 --> 00:19:33.180
robotics platforms, industrial vehicles,

00:19:33.180 --> 00:19:38.180
and anyone who already has a host machine and needs to add sight to it.

00:19:38.180 --> 00:19:42.180
36 crore, the largest of the three compute boxes.

00:19:42.180 --> 00:19:47.180
Together, the A100 family is 90 crore of revenue

00:19:47.180 --> 00:19:51.180
that needs no vehicle, no installer, and no homologation.

00:19:51.180 --> 00:19:56.180
Not hardware at all, a license to the trained perception models and the tool chain

00:19:56.180 --> 00:20:00.180
so a customer can run our software on their own platform,

00:20:00.180 --> 00:20:03.180
priced per deployment with nothing shipped in a box.

00:20:03.180 --> 00:20:08.180
The models are the accumulated result of running these sensors on Indian roads.

00:20:08.180 --> 00:20:12.180
A customer licenses that, integrates it, and ships.

00:20:12.180 --> 00:20:16.180
There is no board, no bill of materials, and nothing to manufacture.

00:20:16.180 --> 00:20:20.180
Teams that want perception tuned for Indian road conditions

00:20:20.180 --> 00:20:23.180
the traffic mix, the lane discipline, the light,

00:20:23.180 --> 00:20:28.180
without spending three years collecting the data to train it themselves.

00:20:28.180 --> 00:20:31.180
The data set is the product here, not the code.

00:20:31.180 --> 00:20:36.180
50 crore at 94% margin and it starts later than everything else

00:20:36.180 --> 00:20:42.180
in the 2029 financial year because a license needs a track record before anyone buys one.

00:20:42.180 --> 00:20:47.180
It is the only line in the plan with no bill of materials whatsoever.

00:20:47.180 --> 00:20:54.180
Two SKUs, 89 crore, and the highest revenue per unit anywhere in the portfolio.

00:20:54.180 --> 00:20:59.180
Here, we stop selling a product and start selling the work the product does.

00:20:59.180 --> 00:21:03.180
Not a sale, a vehicle we own and operate under contract

00:21:03.180 --> 00:21:07.180
where the customer pays for the transport rather than the truck.

00:21:07.180 --> 00:21:12.180
The revenue per unit is the largest in the plan because we are booking the whole job.

00:21:12.180 --> 00:21:16.180
The vehicle runs the same autonomy stack as the kit we sell.

00:21:16.180 --> 00:21:20.180
Fleet logic schedules it, routes it, and hands off to a remote operator

00:21:20.180 --> 00:21:24.180
when the situation exceeds what the vehicle should decide alone.

00:21:24.180 --> 00:21:28.180
The difference from AD2 is commercial, not technical.

00:21:28.180 --> 00:21:33.180
Operators comparing cost per ton kilometer against a driven alternative

00:21:33.180 --> 00:21:38.180
and increasingly comparing against driver availability rather than driver cost

00:21:38.180 --> 00:21:42.180
because on many routes the constraint is finding a driver at all.

00:21:42.180 --> 00:21:47.180
66 crore, it is the line that shows what the technology is worth

00:21:47.180 --> 00:21:51.180
when we capture the operating margin instead of the hardware margin.

00:21:51.180 --> 00:21:55.180
It also carries operational risk the product lines do not.

00:21:55.180 --> 00:21:58.180
We own the asset and the uptime.

00:21:58.180 --> 00:22:02.180
An autonomous container yard vehicle working inside a port perimeter.

00:22:02.180 --> 00:22:05.180
A controlled site but not an easy one.

00:22:05.180 --> 00:22:08.180
Heavy equipment overhead, tight lanes between stacks,

00:22:08.180 --> 00:22:11.180
and real consequences for a positioning error.

00:22:11.180 --> 00:22:17.180
Under a quay crane, satellite positioning degrades exactly where precision matters most.

00:22:17.180 --> 00:22:23.180
Sensor fusion on our silicon holds 7 cm in the open and 22 under the crane

00:22:23.180 --> 00:22:25.180
where satellite alone falls away.

00:22:25.180 --> 00:22:30.180
That positioning is what makes crane synchronized dispatch possible at all.

00:22:30.180 --> 00:22:36.180
Terminal operators measured on throughput and labor cost inside a geofenced site.

00:22:36.180 --> 00:22:38.180
No public road homologation applies,

00:22:38.180 --> 00:22:43.180
so adoption is limited by operational confidence rather than regulation.

00:22:43.180 --> 00:22:49.180
22 and a half crore, read its market share differently from the others.

00:22:49.180 --> 00:22:54.180
The sized pool is total port automation including cranes and software

00:22:54.180 --> 00:22:57.180
and we sell only the vehicle layer.

00:22:57.180 --> 00:23:03.180
A working twin of a container terminal built to prove the three things the program asks for.

00:23:03.180 --> 00:23:06.180
That positioning holds where satellite fails.

00:23:06.180 --> 00:23:08.180
That routing copes with congestion.

00:23:08.180 --> 00:23:11.180
And that scheduling collapses crane idle time.

00:23:11.180 --> 00:23:16.180
It runs in shadow mode first, advising the terminal before it is given control.

00:23:16.180 --> 00:23:21.180
Quay discharge, then the container canyon, then peak mixed traffic.

00:23:21.180 --> 00:23:26.180
Watch the two confidence bars diverge as the vehicle enters the canyon.

00:23:26.180 --> 00:23:29.180
Satellite drops, fusion holds.

00:23:29.180 --> 00:23:32.180
The trial panel on the right carries the numbers.

00:23:32.180 --> 00:23:38.180
Seven centimeters open, 22 under crane, 9% error on arrival time.

00:23:38.180 --> 00:23:42.180
Five SKUs, 83.5 crore.

00:23:42.180 --> 00:23:47.180
Two purposes, sensors that make the main products work in conditions where cameras fail

00:23:47.180 --> 00:23:52.180
and robotics platforms that put the same silicon into a second market cycle.

00:23:52.180 --> 00:23:58.180
A bolt-on long wave infrared pod with its own lens and heater feeding the compute box.

00:23:58.180 --> 00:24:00.180
40,000 rupees.

00:24:00.180 --> 00:24:04.180
It is an accessory to the systems above it, not a stand-alone product.

00:24:04.180 --> 00:24:08.180
It reads emitted heat rather than reflected light.

00:24:08.180 --> 00:24:14.180
So a person or an animal separates from the background in the dark through glare, dust, and smoke.

00:24:14.180 --> 00:24:18.180
The chip runs detection on the thermal image alongside the visible one.

00:24:18.180 --> 00:24:22.180
This is the channel that keeps working when the others stop.

00:24:22.180 --> 00:24:28.180
Fleets running at night and through the monsoon, where camera-only detection degrades sharply.

00:24:28.180 --> 00:24:36.180
It is sold as an upgrade to a system already fitted, which makes it a second sale into an existing customer rather than a new acquisition.

00:24:36.180 --> 00:24:40.180
16 crore at 50% gross margin.

00:24:40.180 --> 00:24:44.180
Half the group average, because a sensor carries real component cost.

00:24:44.180 --> 00:24:50.180
Its job is to raise the capability of the products around it, not to carry the plan.

00:24:50.180 --> 00:24:56.180
A sealed radar pod, mounted forward or on a corner, feeding the same compute box.

00:24:56.180 --> 00:24:58.180
25,000 rupees.

00:24:58.180 --> 00:25:04.180
And weatherproof by design because it has to work in the conditions that defeat everything else.

00:25:04.180 --> 00:25:12.180
It illuminates the scene and reads the returns, and the radar block on the die turns those returns into range, velocity, and angle.

00:25:12.180 --> 00:25:16.180
Tracks are held across frames, including through rain and dust.

00:25:16.180 --> 00:25:22.180
Radar supplies closing speed, the one quantity a camera estimates and a radar measures.

00:25:22.180 --> 00:25:30.180
Operations where weather and dust defeat cameras, and any application where a braking decision has to be defensible afterwards,

00:25:30.180 --> 00:25:36.180
because a measured closing speed stands up where an estimated one does not.

00:25:36.180 --> 00:25:40.180
7.5 crore, the smallest line in the plan.

00:25:40.180 --> 00:25:45.180
It is here because the flagship product needs it, not because it is a business on its own.

00:25:45.180 --> 00:25:50.180
A wrist-worn band with on-body sensors and a radio link to the vehicle.

00:25:50.180 --> 00:25:52.180
1500 rupees.

00:25:52.180 --> 00:25:56.180
It is driver monitoring without a camera pointed at the driver's face.

00:25:56.180 --> 00:25:59.180
The same obligation answered a different way.

00:25:59.180 --> 00:26:02.180
The band reads pulse and movement continuously.

00:26:02.180 --> 00:26:06.180
The vehicle correlates that with how the vehicle is being driven,

00:26:06.180 --> 00:26:09.180
and fatigue is flagged before it becomes a lane departure.

00:26:09.180 --> 00:26:13.180
It measures the driver directly, rather than inferring from the road.

00:26:13.180 --> 00:26:20.180
Fleets meeting driver drowsiness obligations that want a lower intrusion option than an in-cab camera.

00:26:20.180 --> 00:26:25.180
Driver acceptance is a genuine adoption factor, and a wristband clears it more easily.

00:26:25.180 --> 00:26:28.180
10 crore at 50% margin.

00:26:28.180 --> 00:26:32.180
A hedge on how the drowsiness requirement is satisfied in practice,

00:26:32.180 --> 00:26:35.180
on a portfolio that otherwise answers it with a camera.

00:26:35.180 --> 00:26:40.180
An unmanned ground vehicle for defense logistics and reconnaissance.

00:26:40.180 --> 00:26:44.180
It is the only line in the portfolio selling to a government customer,

00:26:44.180 --> 00:26:49.180
and the only one whose buying cycle is measured in years, rather than quarters.

00:26:49.180 --> 00:26:53.180
The same fusion stack pointed outward instead of forward.

00:26:53.180 --> 00:27:00.180
Radar, thermal, and camera tracks along a perimeter are classified individually, then correlated.

00:27:00.180 --> 00:27:07.180
So a vehicle loitering on an access road and a person approaching the fence stop being two unrelated contacts.

00:27:07.180 --> 00:27:13.180
Defense procurement. For tasks where sending a vehicle is preferable to sending people.

00:27:13.180 --> 00:27:20.180
Qualification is long, and the cycles are slow, but once a platform is in, it is very hard to displace.

00:27:20.180 --> 00:27:23.180
30 crore at 68% margin.

00:27:23.180 --> 00:27:28.180
Its real value is strategic, a sovereign customer, a separate procurement route,

00:27:28.180 --> 00:27:32.180
and a use case that does not depend on the automotive mandate at all.

00:27:32.180 --> 00:27:39.180
A live perimeter console. It correlates a vehicle track with a human track into one threat,

00:27:39.180 --> 00:27:44.180
escalates it along a timed ladder from loiter to approach to breach risk,

00:27:44.180 --> 00:27:48.180
and hands the target to ground sensors, a patrol drone or a sea buoy.

00:27:48.180 --> 00:27:51.180
The feature that matters is the restraint,

00:27:51.180 --> 00:27:54.180
but nine tracks are held and never escalated,

00:27:54.180 --> 00:27:58.180
because false alarm rate is what gets a perimeter system switched off.

00:27:58.180 --> 00:28:04.180
Coordinated intrusion, then perimeter patrol, then a maritime approach.

00:28:04.180 --> 00:28:11.180
Watch two separate tracks fuse into one threat, and watch the escalation ladder on the right fill in.

00:28:11.180 --> 00:28:16.180
The operator sees intent forming, rather than an alarm at the breach.

00:28:16.180 --> 00:28:19.180
The console states its reasoning in words.

00:28:19.180 --> 00:28:24.180
A flight-capable compute and perception kit sold to airframe builders,

00:28:24.180 --> 00:28:28.180
it is the smallest and lightest configuration of the same silicon,

00:28:28.180 --> 00:28:31.180
constrained by weight and power rather than by cost.

00:28:31.180 --> 00:28:36.180
Cameras and an inertial unit read the scene and the airframe's own motion.

00:28:36.180 --> 00:28:40.180
The chip holds position and detects obstacles onboard,

00:28:40.180 --> 00:28:44.180
so the aircraft is not dependent on a radio link to stay safe.

00:28:44.180 --> 00:28:46.180
The flight controller executes.

00:28:46.180 --> 00:28:50.180
Airframe builders with onboard autonomy requirements,

00:28:50.180 --> 00:28:56.180
inspection, survey, and any mission flown where the radio link cannot be relied upon.

00:28:56.180 --> 00:29:01.180
They buy compute, not aircraft, and they build the rest themselves.

00:29:01.180 --> 00:29:02.180
Twenty crore.

00:29:02.180 --> 00:29:06.180
Treat it as optionality rather than base case.

00:29:06.180 --> 00:29:10.180
The same dye, in a market with a different cycle and different buyers,

00:29:10.180 --> 00:29:13.180
entered without designing new silicon to get there.

00:29:13.180 --> 00:29:15.180
Fifteen products explained.

00:29:15.180 --> 00:29:17.180
Now what they do together?

00:29:17.180 --> 00:29:21.180
Concentration, timing, where the autonomy actually runs,

00:29:21.180 --> 00:29:26.180
where the margin is, and how much of each market we are assuming we win.

00:29:26.180 --> 00:29:28.180
Be clear about this.

00:29:28.180 --> 00:29:31.180
Two SKUs carry 64%.

00:29:31.180 --> 00:29:36.180
The truck kit and the smart mirror are the same product family, sold to overlapping buyers.

00:29:36.180 --> 00:29:42.180
So a certification delay or an installation bottleneck hits 720 crore at once,

00:29:42.180 --> 00:29:45.180
more than the other 13 SKUs combined.

00:29:45.180 --> 00:29:48.180
That is the single largest risk in the plan.

00:29:48.180 --> 00:29:54.180
Six SKUs earn revenue in the 2025 and financial year, nine more in 2028.

00:29:54.180 --> 00:30:01.180
The 2027 revenue is 1.7 crore across six products on current generation hardware,

00:30:01.180 --> 00:30:02.180
deliberately small.

00:30:02.180 --> 00:30:06.180
The ramp switches on when production silicon reaches market,

00:30:06.180 --> 00:30:09.180
and the license line starts later still.

00:30:09.180 --> 00:30:13.180
The shape of this plan is gated by the chip, which is what the round funds.

00:30:14.180 --> 00:30:20.180
86% of revenue runs outdoors, where public road rules and homologation apply.

00:30:20.180 --> 00:30:27.180
6% runs indoors, geofenced, where Level 4 ships years earlier on identical silicon.

00:30:27.180 --> 00:30:31.180
8% is pure compute with no vehicle exposure at all.

00:30:31.180 --> 00:30:35.180
Same chip, three very different regulatory positions.

00:30:35.180 --> 00:30:41.180
Volume and margin sit in different places, and that is the useful thing on this slide.

00:30:41.180 --> 00:30:46.180
Systems are 83% of revenue, so group economics tracks systems,

00:30:46.180 --> 00:30:51.180
but the marginal rupee is worth most in semiconductors at 94%,

00:30:51.180 --> 00:30:54.180
and those lines need no installation capacity.

00:30:54.180 --> 00:31:00.180
Growth in the silicon SKUs improves group margin without adding operational load.

00:31:00.180 --> 00:31:04.180
No skew in this plan assumes we win its market.

00:31:04.180 --> 00:31:08.180
Every line sits under 2% of its own size demand pool,

00:31:08.180 --> 00:31:12.180
and most pools are global, while our addressable market is the India slice,

00:31:12.180 --> 00:31:15.180
so the real share is higher than shown.

00:31:15.180 --> 00:31:17.180
Read the port line differently.

00:31:17.180 --> 00:31:22.180
That pool is total port automation, and we sell only the vehicle layer.

00:31:22.180 --> 00:31:26.180
One more part, and deliberately not the one this raises funds.

00:31:26.180 --> 00:31:33.180
SOC4A is the far horizon, 1,024 tiles on a 4 nanometer process,

00:31:33.180 --> 00:31:38.180
four petaflops at FP4, assembled from published silicon-proven blocks

00:31:38.180 --> 00:31:40.180
from the open pulp platform.

00:31:40.180 --> 00:31:43.180
It is optionality that sits outside this plan.

00:31:43.180 --> 00:31:45.180
SOC2 is the business.

00:31:45.180 --> 00:31:48.180
This is the option the business buys you.

00:31:48.180 --> 00:31:51.180
Fifteen products, one tapeout.

00:31:51.180 --> 00:31:53.180
The demand is legislated.

00:31:53.180 --> 00:31:57.180
The margin sits in silicon that needs no installation capacity,

00:31:57.180 --> 00:32:02.180
and no line in the plan requires us to win its market.

00:32:02.180 --> 00:32:07.180
Release the capital against silicon, certification, and paying customers,

00:32:07.180 --> 00:32:09.180
not against the forecast.

00:32:09.180 --> 00:32:12.180
The portfolio is not 15 bets.

00:32:12.180 --> 00:32:15.180
It is one bet, 15 times over.
